## Uneven widths — widths="2 1 1"

The first column takes twice the width of the other two. Content that scales
(an SVG, or text) fills its column; a small raster renders at its natural
size within the column.

{% columns widths="2 1 1" %}
* ![](swatch.svg)
* ![](swatch.svg)
* ![](swatch.svg)
{% /columns %}

## Captions — above, below, and coloured

Put a `\{% caption %}` and its image in one list item, separated by a blank
line, so they stay distinct blocks. `position="below"` moves the caption
under the image, and `color="#…"` tints it (or set `caption_color` in the
style to colour every caption):

{% columns %}
* {% caption %}Above (the default){% /caption %}

  ![](swatch.png)

* {% caption position="below" color="#c026d3" %}Below, coloured{% /caption %}

  ![](swatch.svg)
{% /columns %}

## Text flanking a centred image — widths="2 1 2"

A wide outer pair of text lanes around a narrow image lane centres a figure
with commentary on either side. Each lane is its own stream, so the two
sides are independent (they can differ), and — like any columns — they keep
their width for the whole row rather than reflowing below the image. For
text that wraps down *one* side of an image and then widens beneath it, use
`\{% float %}` instead.

{% columns widths="2 1 2" gap=18 %}
* Left-hand text runs down this side of the figure. It is its own lane,
  independent of the text on the right, so neither side crosses the image
  and neither widens below it.
* ![](figure.svg)
* Right-hand text runs down the other side. Because each lane is its own
  stream, the two sides can carry different commentary at once — a figure
  annotated on the left and the right at the same time.
{% /columns %}

## A figure panel — background and centered content

`align="center"` centres each column's content — the image and the text
beneath it — and `background="#…"` fills a panel behind the whole row. Give a
cell an image, a bold headline, and a body paragraph (blank-line separated),
and it reads as a captioned figure:

{% columns gap=20 align="center" background="#f2f3f5" %}
* ![](figure.svg)

  **Indicator released**

  If the input pins deliver 24 V, the unit can operate.

* ![](figure.svg)

  **Indicator engaged**

  When both pins deliver 0 V, the unit stops and the housing locks.
{% /columns %}

## Responsive grid

`\{% grid %}` takes the same cells as `\{% columns %}` but *reflows* them into
as many equal columns as fit at `min` points wide (default 120), wrapping into
rows — the print analogue of CSS `repeat(auto-fill, minmax(min, 1fr))`. `gap`
spaces both columns and rows; `align` and `background` work as above. A short
final row is left-packed, its cells the same width as the rows above. Here six
cells settle into a three-column grid:

{% grid min=110 gap=16 align="center" %}
* ![](swatch.png)

  **Red**

  Emergency or protective stop

* ![](swatch.png)

  **Green**

  Ready for job

* ![](swatch.png)

  **Cyan**

  Driving to destination

* ![](swatch.png)

  **Purple**

  Goal or path blocked

* ![](swatch.png)

  **Blue**

  Manual drive

* ![](swatch.png)

  **Orange**

  Mission paused
{% /grid %}
